I'm the first to admit I don't know anything about phone hardware, but
I just went to the website of a few phone makers websites, and I can't
see what the problem is.  Top end phones with keyboards, personal
organisers, Blackberrys and that sort of thing can have 32, 64 or 128
megs of memory, some have ram only, but others have a mixture of ram
and flash.
Even a normal phone can have a display resolution of 200x200, bigger
items have screens from 320 to 600 pixels wide.

How much ram, how much flash memory would a phone need for nethack?

And what do you think about resolution?  What would be the bare
minimum, and what would be ideal?

I think a very ugly version of ascii could be put into a 5x5 pixel
font, so a full map display of this would only need 400x250.  If only a
portion of the map was displayed, the screen could be even smaller, or
graphics could be used.  200 by 200 is possible (not good, but
possible) to play nethack on, so I don't think screen size is a problem
these days.

So what is the stumbling block?  Memory? Processing power?  OS?
Phones are more powerful than desktop computers of
yester-year.....aren't they?



